      "id": "https://nautil.us/take-a-look-at-the-countrys-newest-national-park-1286101/",
      "url": "https://nautil.us/take-a-look-at-the-countrys-newest-national-park-1286101/",
      "title": "[nautil.us] Arizona’s Chiricahua becomes U.S. national park after congressional act",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://kagiproxy.com/img/iel1DR4ETB0L03cRWrcqixsHWGLEBFG-YfJRqpxHffLbu_b0JLdbst_UTyKhzSbyjJ0vPp0Zi0aBxbRp88yhuwRs_LLcW_tM8FKQiruogJYFnieniDNCKXvsprisVIplrbD0aJP9ZTFu1SxqZSo\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🪶 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnautil.us%2Ftake-a-look-at-the-countrys-newest-national-park-1286101%2F\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\nChiricahua in southeastern Arizona became the United States’ 64th national park in October, after Congress passed a bill and President Donald J. Trump signed it into law. The 12,000-acre area had been a national monument for more than a century.\n\nThe park’s rock spires and canyons formed as erosion wore away volcanic deposits left by eruptions about 27 million years ago. The new designation covers a landscape with a long human history and is expected to draw more visitors, who tend to stay overnight more often at national parks than at monuments.",
